# The pinned Windows SDK's headers and x64 libraries, as Microsoft's own NuGet
# packages, with the digest of each.
#
# Three whitespace-separated columns. Lines starting with # are comments.
#
#   <nuget-package-id>  <sha256-of-the-nupkg>  <destination>
#
# The version comes from build/WINDOWS_SDK_INSTALLER_VERSION, so the headers,
# the libraries and the Debugging Tools are all one revision by construction and
# there is no second version to keep in step. The URL is derived from the id and
# the version: nuget.org's flat container is
# https://api.nuget.org/v3-flatcontainer/<id>/<version>/<id>.<version>.nupkg
#
# <destination> says where the package's payload is overlaid, relative to the
# SDK root, with <version> replaced by build/WINDOWS_SDK_VERSION:
#
#   include   c/Include/<version>/  ->  <SDK>/Include/<version>/
#   lib-x64   c/um/x64/ and c/ucrt/x64/  ->  <SDK>/Lib/<version>/{um,ucrt}/x64/
#
# WHY NOT THE SDK INSTALLER
#
# Because it does not finish. Measured on blacksmith-2vcpu-windows-2025:
# winsdksetup.exe asked for OptionId.DesktopCPPx64, OptionId.DesktopCPPx86 and
# OptionId.WindowsDesktopDebuggers ran the full 25-minute bound and had reached
# only package 004, exit 124. It was making progress -- slow, not stuck -- but a
# quarter of the gate's 180-minute budget spent before the first compile, every
# run, is not a cost worth paying for a file copy. For contrast on the same
# runner: the ATL component through the VS installer takes 26 seconds, and the
# Debuggers feature alone through this same winsdksetup takes 8, which is why
# the Debuggers still come from it.
#
# Measured for the overlay on the same runner class: both packages fetched,
# digest-checked and copied in 15 seconds, and the whole SDK phase including the
# Debuggers install in 24. The Include and Lib trees still list only
# 10.0.22621.0 and 10.0.26100.0 afterwards, so this upgrades in place and does
# not leave two revisions side by side.
#
# The packages are also better provenance than the installer, not worse.
# winsdksetup.exe is a 1.4 MB bootstrapper: we can pin its bytes, and then it
# downloads whatever the service gives it. These are the payload itself, pinned
# by digest, so what lands on the runner is what this file names.
#
# WHAT IS AND IS NOT COVERED
#
# The x86 libraries are deliberately not overlaid, so <SDK>/Lib/<version>/um/x86
# stays at whatever revision the image carries. Nothing links x86 here: no
# windows-x86 target exists, and the only reason an x86 toolchain is configured
# at all is that build/toolchain/win/BUILD.gn instantiates win_toolchains("x86")
# alongside x64, whose setup only requires the directories to EXIST. A mixed
# revision is worth naming rather than hiding, and this is where it is named.
#
# The libraries are overlaid and not only the headers, because the headers alone
# would compile and then fail at link. um/UIAutomationCore.h declares
# EXTERN_C const CLSID CLSID_CUIAutomationClientInfoSource, which is DEFINED in
# a library -- found in this package's c/um/x64/Uuid.Lib -- and
# ui/accessibility/platform/uia_client_info_source_win.cc:144 passes it to
# CoCreateInstance. The series compile gate never links, so the gate would not
# have caught that and a nightly build would have.

